    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1062">

                <pb facs="1239" n="1043" sameAs="frus:frus1945Berlinv02/pg1043"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">Department of the Army Files: Telegram</note>
                <head>No. 1062<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of
                            State to the Ambassador in France</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                    >Caffery</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">top secret</hi>
                        <lb/>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">priority</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Babelsberg</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1945-07-20">July 20, 1945</date>.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Victory</hi> 116.
                    For the Ambassador from the Secretary of State.</p>
                <p>I have today<anchor n="1" xml:id="d1062-f1"/> addressed a letter to Molotov proposing Soviet participation in
                    supervising Greek elections along the lines of the text proposed in the
                    Department’s telegram of July 17,<anchor n="2" xml:id="d1062-f2"/> which was
                    repeated to you. I would appreciate your making a similar proposal to the French
                    Government immediately.</p>
                <p>The British Delegation suggests, and I concur, that no publicity whatever be
                    given to this proposal until replies are received from the French and Soviet
                    Governments and the Greek Government can be appropriately informed.</p>
                <p>Sent to Paris. Repeated to Washington and Athens.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
